{ L"What is 25 \u00D7 48?", 1200, L"Regroup: 25 \u00D7 4 = 100 and 48 = 4 \u00D7 12, so 100 \u00D7 12 = 1200." },
{ L"What is 98 \u00D7 102?", 9996, L"(100 \u2212 2)(100 + 2) = 100\u00B2 \u2212 2\u00B2 = 10000 \u2212 4." },
{ L"What is 0.125 \u00D7 888?", 111, L"0.125 = 1/8, and 888 / 8 = 111." },
{ L"What is \u221A(3\u00B2 + 4\u00B2 + 12\u00B2)?", 13, L"9 + 16 + 144 = 169, and 169 = 13\u00B2." },
{ L"What is 37 \u00D7 27?", 999, L"27 = 3 \u00D7 9 and 37 \u00D7 3 = 111, so 37 \u00D7 27 = 111 \u00D7 9 = 999." },
{ L"What is 47\u00B2 \u2212 43\u00B2?", 360, L"Difference of squares: (47 \u2212 43)(47 + 43) = 4 \u00D7 90 = 360." },
{ L"What is 2026\u00B2 \u2212 2025 \u00D7 2027?", 1, L"2025 \u00D7 2027 = (2026 \u2212 1)(2026 + 1) = 2026\u00B2 \u2212 1, so the difference is 1." },
{ L"What is 101 \u00D7 102 \u2212 100 \u00D7 103?", 2, L"With n = 100: (n + 1)(n + 2) \u2212 n(n + 3) = (n\u00B2 + 3n + 2) \u2212 (n\u00B2 + 3n) = 2." },
{ L"What is 100 \u2212 99 + 98 \u2212 97 + \u2026 + 4 \u2212 3 + 2 \u2212 1?", 50, L"Group in pairs: (100 \u2212 99) + (98 \u2212 97) + \u2026 + (2 \u2212 1) is 50 pairs, each equal to 1." },
{ L"What is 1234 + 2341 + 3412 + 4123?", 11110, L"Each digit 1, 2, 3, 4 appears once in every place, so the sum is (1 + 2 + 3 + 4) \u00D7 1111 = 11110." },
{ L"What is 999 \u00D7 999?", 998001, L"(1000 \u2212 1)\u00B2 = 1000000 \u2212 2000 + 1 = 998001." },
{ L"What is 12345 \u00D7 9 + 6?", 111111, L"12345 \u00D7 9 = 123450 \u2212 12345 = 111105, and adding 6 gives 111111." },
{ L"What is 1 + 2 + 3 + \u2026 + 99 + 100 + 99 + \u2026 + 3 + 2 + 1?", 10000, L"Pair k on the way up with 100 \u2212 k on the way down: 99 pairs of 100, plus the middle 100, give 100 \u00D7 100 = 10000." },
{ L"What is 1000 \u00D7 (1 \u2212 1/2) \u00D7 (1 \u2212 1/3) \u00D7 (1 \u2212 1/4) \u00D7 \u2026 \u00D7 (1 \u2212 1/100)?", 10, L"Each factor is (k \u2212 1)/k, so 1/2 \u00D7 2/3 \u00D7 \u2026 \u00D7 99/100 telescopes to 1/100. Then 1000/100 = 10." },
{ L"What is \u221A(10 \u00D7 11 \u00D7 12 \u00D7 13 + 1)?", 131, L"10 \u00D7 13 = 130 and 11 \u00D7 12 = 132, so the product plus 1 is 130 \u00D7 132 + 1 = (131 \u2212 1)(131 + 1) + 1 = 131\u00B2." },
{ L"What is the smallest positive whole number divisible by 4, 6, 9 and 10?", 180, L"Take the highest prime powers needed: 2\u00B2 (from 4), 3\u00B2 (from 9) and 5 (from 10). 4 \u00D7 9 \u00D7 5 = 180." },
{ L"What is the smallest four-digit number divisible by 7, 11 and 13?", 1001, L"7, 11 and 13 are primes, so it must be a multiple of 7 \u00D7 11 \u00D7 13 = 1001, which already has four digits." },
{ L"What is the sum of all positive divisors of 28, not counting 28 itself?", 28, L"1 + 2 + 4 + 7 + 14 = 28. A number equal to the sum of its other divisors is called perfect." },
{ L"What is the largest prime factor of 999?", 37, L"999 = 27 \u00D7 37 = 3\u00B3 \u00D7 37, and 37 is prime." },
{ L"What is the greatest common divisor of 1001 and 1309?", 77, L"1001 = 7 \u00D7 11 \u00D7 13 and 1309 = 7 \u00D7 11 \u00D7 17. The shared part is 7 \u00D7 11 = 77." },
{ L"What is the last digit of 7 to the power 100?", 1, L"Last digits of powers of 7 cycle 7, 9, 3, 1 every 4 steps. 100 is a multiple of 4, so it ends in 1." },
{ L"How many positive divisors does 72 have, including 1 and 72?", 12, L"72 = 2\u00B3 \u00D7 3\u00B2. A divisor uses 0 to 3 twos and 0 to 2 threes: 4 \u00D7 3 = 12 choices." },
{ L"How many prime numbers are there between 90 and 110?", 5, L"Only odd numbers not divisible by 3 or 5 can be prime: 91 = 7 \u00D7 13 fails; 97, 101, 103, 107, 109 are prime." },
{ L"How many whole numbers from 1 to 100 are divisible by 3 or by 5 (or both)?", 47, L"Inclusion-exclusion: 33 multiples of 3 + 20 multiples of 5 \u2212 6 multiples of 15 = 47." },
{ L"Using only 3-cent and 5-cent stamps, what is the largest postage in cents that cannot be made exactly?", 7, L"8 = 3 + 5, 9 = 3 + 3 + 3, 10 = 5 + 5, and adding 3s reaches every larger amount. But 7 cannot be made." },
{ L"What is the largest whole number that divides n \u00D7 (n + 1) \u00D7 (n + 2) for every positive whole number n?", 6, L"Among 3 consecutive numbers one is even and one is a multiple of 3, so 6 always divides. n = 1 gives exactly 1 \u00D7 2 \u00D7 3 = 6." },
{ L"With n! = 1 \u00D7 2 \u00D7 \u2026 \u00D7 n, what is the last digit of 1! + 2! + 3! + \u2026 + 100!?", 3, L"From the 5th term on, each factorial contains 2 \u00D7 5, so it ends in 0. 1 + 2 + 6 + 24 = 33, so the last digit is 3." },
{ L"How many zeros are at the end of 100! = 1 \u00D7 2 \u00D7 3 \u00D7 \u2026 \u00D7 100?", 24, L"Each zero needs a 5 (2s are plentiful): 20 multiples of 5, plus 4 extra 5s from 25, 50, 75, 100. 20 + 4 = 24." },
{ L"What is the remainder when 123456789 is divided by 11?", 5, L"Test for 11: alternate signs from the right. 9 \u2212 8 + 7 \u2212 6 + 5 \u2212 4 + 3 \u2212 2 + 1 = 5." },
{ L"What is the smallest positive whole number whose digits multiply to 100?", 455, L"Two digits give at most 9 \u00D7 9 = 81, so use three. 100 = 4 \u00D7 5 \u00D7 5, smallest digit first: 455." },
{ L"What are the last two digits of 7 to the power 2026? Type them as a two-digit number.", 49, L"7^4 = 2401 ends in 01, so the last two digits repeat every 4 powers. 2026 = 4 \u00D7 506 + 2, so it ends like 7\u00B2 = 49." },
{ L"What is the smallest positive whole number with exactly 10 positive divisors?", 48, L"Exactly 10 divisors means p^9 or p^4 \u00D7 q for primes p, q. Smallest is 2^4 \u00D7 3 = 48 (2^9 = 512 is bigger)." },
{ L"What is the smallest positive whole number that leaves remainder 1 when divided by 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6, but is divisible by 7?", 301, L"It must be 1 more than a multiple of 60: 61, 121, 181, 241, 301. The first divisible by 7 is 301 = 7 \u00D7 43." },
{ L"The number N is written as fifty 9s in a row. What is the sum of the digits of N\u00B2?", 450, L"Like 999\u00B2 = 998001, N\u00B2 is 49 nines, an 8, 49 zeros and a 1. Digit sum 49 \u00D7 9 + 8 + 1 = 450." },
{ L"What is the largest whole number that divides n\u00B2 \u2212 1 for every odd whole number n greater than 1?", 8, L"n\u00B2 \u2212 1 = (n \u2212 1)(n + 1): two consecutive even numbers, one a multiple of 4, so 8 divides. n = 3 gives exactly 8." },
{ L"What number comes next: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, \u2026?", 34, L"Each term is the sum of the two before it: 13 + 21 = 34." },
{ L"What number comes next: 1, 2, 4, 7, 11, 16, \u2026?", 22, L"The gaps grow by one: +1, +2, +3, +4, +5, so next is 16 + 6 = 22." },
{ L"What number comes next: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, 42, \u2026?", 56, L"The terms are 1 \u00D7 2, 2 \u00D7 3, 3 \u00D7 4, \u2026, 6 \u00D7 7, so next is 7 \u00D7 8 = 56." },
{ L"What is 2 + 4 + 6 + \u2026 + 100, the sum of the first 50 even numbers?", 2550, L"It is 2 \u00D7 (1 + 2 + \u2026 + 50) = 2 \u00D7 1275 = 2550. Or: 50 terms averaging 51." },
{ L"What is the 100th term of the sequence 3, 7, 11, 15, 19, \u2026?", 399, L"Start at 3 and add 4 ninety-nine times: 3 + 99 \u00D7 4 = 399." },
{ L"What is 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + \u2026 + 512, where each term is double the one before?", 1023, L"Adding 1 snowballs: 1 + 1 = 2, 2 + 2 = 4, \u2026, 512 + 512 = 1024. So the sum is 1024 \u2212 1 = 1023." },
{ L"What number comes next: 3, 5, 9, 17, 33, \u2026?", 65, L"Each term is a power of 2 plus 1 (2 + 1, 4 + 1, \u2026, 32 + 1). Next is 64 + 1 = 65." },
{ L"What number comes next: 1, 4, 27, 256, \u2026?", 3125, L"The terms are 1^1, 2\u00B2, 3\u00B3, 4^4, so next is 5^5 = 3125." },
{ L"What number comes next: 1, 11, 121, 1331, \u2026?", 14641, L"These are powers of 11: 11^0, 11^1, 11\u00B2, 11\u00B3. Next is 11^4 = 14641." },
{ L"Each term is the sum of the two before it: 2, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11, \u2026 What is the 10th term?", 76, L"Keep adding the last two: 2, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11, 18, 29, 47, 76. The 10th term is 76." },
{ L"1/7 = 0.142857142857\u2026 What is the 2026th digit after the decimal point?", 8, L"1/7 repeats every 6 digits. 2026 = 6 \u00D7 337 + 4, so take the 4th digit of 142857: 8." },
{ L"What number comes next: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 23, 28, 38, 49, \u2026? Hint: look at the digits of each term.", 62, L"Each term adds its own digit sum: 16 + 7 = 23, 23 + 5 = 28, 38 + 11 = 49, so next is 49 + 13 = 62." },
{ L"In the sequence 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, \u2026 each number n appears n times. What is the 100th term?", 14, L"Numbers 1 to n fill n(n + 1)/2 places. Up to 13 fills 91, up to 14 fills 105, so place 100 holds 14." },
{ L"What is 1\u00B3 + 2\u00B3 + 3\u00B3 + \u2026 + 10\u00B3?", 3025, L"The sum of the first n cubes is (1 + 2 + \u2026 + n)\u00B2. Here (1 + \u2026 + 10)\u00B2 = 55\u00B2 = 3025." },
{ L"Writing all positive whole numbers in a row gives 123456789101112\u2026 What is the 100th digit of this row?", 5, L"1 to 9 use 9 digits, leaving 91. Numbers 10 to 54 use 90 more, so the 100th digit is the first digit of 55." },
{ L"What number comes next: 1, 11, 21, 1211, 111221, \u2026? Hint: read each term out loud.", 312211, L"Each term describes the one before: 111221 is three 1s, two 2s, one 1, written 312211." },
{ L"A drawer has 10 red and 10 blue socks. In the dark, how many must you take out to be sure of a matching pair?", 3, L"There are only 2 colors, so among any 3 socks two must match." },
{ L"100 players enter a knockout tournament. Each match knocks out exactly one player. How many matches are needed to find the winner?", 99, L"Each match eliminates one player, and 99 players must be eliminated." },
{ L"In a line of 25 people, Ann is 9th from the front and Bob is 8th from the back. How many people stand between Ann and Bob?", 8, L"Bob is 8th from the back, which is 25 \u2212 8 + 1 = 18th from the front. Places 10 to 17 lie between: 8 people." },
{ L"A drawer has 10 red and 10 blue socks. In the dark, how many must you take out to be sure of getting two blue socks?", 12, L"Worst case you draw all 10 red socks first. Then the next 2 must be blue: 10 + 2 = 12." },
{ L"A drawer has 5 black, 7 white and 9 grey socks. In the dark, how many must you take out to be sure of 3 socks of one color?", 7, L"Worst case is 2 of each color, 6 socks. The 7th sock makes 3 of some color." },
{ L"Of 100 politicians, at least one is honest, and of any two, at least one is corrupt. How many of them are corrupt?", 99, L"If two were honest, that pair would break the rule. So exactly 1 is honest and 99 are corrupt." },
{ L"How many times is the digit 7 written when you write down all whole numbers from 1 to 100?", 20, L"10 in the ones place (7, 17, \u2026, 97) and 10 in the tens place (70 to 79). 77 counts in both, since it has two 7s." },
{ L"How many digits are printed in total when numbering the pages of a 200-page book from 1 to 200?", 492, L"Pages 1 to 9 use 9 digits, 10 to 99 use 90 \u00D7 2 = 180, and 100 to 200 use 101 \u00D7 3 = 303. Total 492." },
{ L"What is the smallest number of people needed to be sure that at least 3 of them have their birthday in the same month?", 25, L"With 12 months, 24 people can be just 2 per month. The 25th forces a third into some month." },
{ L"A snail is at the bottom of a 10 m well. Each day it climbs 3 m, each night it slides back 2 m. On which day does it first reach the top?", 8, L"It nets 1 m per day, but it does not slide back once out. After 7 days it is at 7 m, and on day 8 it climbs 3 m to the top." },
{ L"10 bags hold 10 coins each. Real coins weigh 10 g; one bag holds only 11 g fakes. What is the fewest uses of a digital scale that always finds that bag?", 1, L"Take 1 coin from bag 1, 2 from bag 2, \u2026, 10 from bag 10. Weigh all 55 once: the grams over 550 give the fake bag." },
{ L"A, B, C are each a knight (always truthful) or a knave (always lies). A: B is a knave. B: C is a knave. C: A and B are both knaves. How many are knights?", 1, L"If C were a knight, A and B are knaves, but then A's claim is true. So C is a knave, B is a knight, A is a knave." },
{ L"Three people are each a knight (always truthful) or a knave (always lies). Each of them says: Exactly two of us are knaves. How many knights are there?", 0, L"If anyone were a knight, the claim is true, so the knaves would say something true. So all 3 are knaves, and the claim is false." },
{ L"A list has 100 statements. For each k from 1 to 100, statement k says: Exactly k statements in this list are false. How many statements are true?", 1, L"The statements contradict each other, so at most one is true. If none were, all 100 are false and statement 100 is true. So exactly one: statement 99." },
{ L"100 lockers, numbered 1 to 100, start closed. For each k from 1 to 100, someone toggles every locker whose number is a multiple of k. How many end open?", 10, L"Locker n is toggled once per divisor of n. Only perfect squares have an odd number of divisors: 1, 4, 9, \u2026, 100, which is 10." },
{ L"Of 80 identical-looking coins, one is heavier than the others. What is the fewest weighings on a two-pan balance that always finds it?", 4, L"Each weighing has 3 outcomes (left, right, balance), so it can cut the suspects to a third. 27 < 80 \u2264 81 = 3^4, so 4 weighings." },
{ L"10 people sit at a round table, each a knight (always truthful) or a knave (always lies). Each says: Both my neighbors are knaves. At least how many are knights?", 4, L"Every knave needs a knight neighbor, and a knight covers only itself and 2 neighbors, so 3 knights cover at most 9 seats. Seats 1, 4, 7, 9 work." },
{ L"A book costs 10 dollars plus half of its own price. How many dollars does the book cost?", 20, L"p = 10 + p/2, so p/2 = 10 and p = 20. The 10 dollars is the other half of the price." },
{ L"If 5 machines make 5 widgets in 5 minutes, how many minutes do 100 machines need to make 100 widgets?", 5, L"Each machine makes 1 widget in 5 minutes, so 100 machines make 100 widgets in the same 5 minutes." },
{ L"Alice paints a fence in 3 hours and Bob paints it in 6 hours. Working together, how many minutes do they need?", 120, L"Per hour they paint 1/3 + 1/6 = 1/2 of the fence, so it takes 2 hours, which is 120 minutes." },
{ L"A father is 4 times as old as his son. In 20 years he will be twice as old as his son. How old is the son now?", 10, L"Father 4s, son s: 4s + 20 = 2(s + 20), so 2s = 20 and s = 10." },
{ L"A purse holds equally many 1-, 2- and 5-cent coins, worth 96 cents in total. How many coins are in the purse?", 36, L"One coin of each kind is worth 1 + 2 + 5 = 8 cents. 96 / 8 = 12 sets, so 3 \u00D7 12 = 36 coins." },
{ L"I have 10 coins, each a 2-cent or a 5-cent coin, worth 41 cents in total. How many 5-cent coins do I have?", 7, L"Ten 2-cent coins make 20 cents. Each swap to a 5-cent coin adds 3, and 41 \u2212 20 = 21 = 7 \u00D7 3." },
{ L"You drive 60 km at 30 km/h, then return the same 60 km at 60 km/h. What is your average speed for the round trip, in km/h?", 40, L"Average speed is total distance over total time: 120 km in 2 + 1 = 3 hours, so 40 km/h, not 45." },
{ L"Trains 300 km apart head toward each other at 70 and 80 km/h. A bird flies back and forth between them at 100 km/h until they meet. How many km does it fly?", 200, L"The trains close the gap at 70 + 80 = 150 km/h, so they meet after 2 hours. The bird flies 100 \u00D7 2 = 200 km." },
{ L"How many liters of water must be added to 10 liters of a 30% salt solution to make it a 20% salt solution?", 5, L"There are 3 liters of salt. At 20% that needs 3 / 0.2 = 15 liters in total, so add 5." },
{ L"A tap fills a tub in 12 minutes; the open drain empties it in 20 minutes. With both open, how many minutes does it take to fill the empty tub?", 30, L"Per minute the tub gains 1/12 \u2212 1/20 = 5/60 \u2212 3/60 = 1/30 of its volume, so it takes 30 minutes." },
{ L"A 100 m long train passes a pole in 5 seconds. How many seconds does it take to completely cross a 300 m bridge?", 20, L"The train moves 100 m in 5 s, so 20 m/s. To clear the bridge it travels 300 + 100 = 400 m: 400/20 = 20 seconds." },
{ L"Runners at 5 m/s and 3 m/s start together on a 400 m track, going the same way. After how many seconds does the faster one first lap the slower?", 200, L"The faster one gains 5 \u2212 3 = 2 m/s and must gain a full 400 m lap: 400/2 = 200 seconds." },
{ L"A boat goes 24 km downstream in 2 hours and back upstream in 3 hours. What is the speed of the current in km/h?", 2, L"Downstream 12 km/h is boat + current, upstream 8 km/h is boat \u2212 current. Current = (12 \u2212 8)/2 = 2." },
{ L"A price rises by 25%, then the new price drops by 20%. By how many percent does the final price differ from the original?", 0, L"1.25 \u00D7 0.8 = 1, so the final price equals the original." },
{ L"The ages of a mother and her daughter add up to 50. Five years ago the mother was 7 times as old as the daughter. How old is the mother now?", 40, L"Five years ago their ages summed to 40 and were in the ratio 7 to 1: 35 and 5. So the mother is now 40." },
{ L"100 kg of cucumbers are 99% water. After drying in the sun they are 98% water. How many kg do they weigh now?", 50, L"The 1 kg of dry matter stays. Now it is 2% of the weight, so the total is 1/0.02 = 50 kg." },
{ L"Ann is 24. She is twice as old as Ben was when Ann was as old as Ben is now. How old is Ben now?", 18, L"Let Ben be b. The age gap is 24 \u2212 b, so back then Ben was 2b \u2212 24. Then 24 = 2(2b \u2212 24), giving b = 18." },
{ L"Walking up a stopped escalator takes 60 seconds. Standing on it while it moves takes 40 seconds. How many seconds does walking up the moving one take?", 24, L"Each second you climb 1/60 of it and the escalator moves 1/40. Together 1/60 + 1/40 = 1/24 per second: 24 seconds." },
{ L"A hen and a half lays an egg and a half in a day and a half. At that rate, how many eggs do 6 hens lay in 6 days?", 24, L"Divide by 1.5: one hen lays one egg in 1.5 days, so 4 eggs in 6 days. 6 hens lay 6 \u00D7 4 = 24." },
{ L"A 40 liter barrel is 25% orange juice and 75% water. How many liters of pure orange juice must be added to make it 40% orange juice?", 10, L"The 30 liters of water stay and must become 60%, so the total is 30/0.6 = 50 liters. Add 50 \u2212 40 = 10." },
{ L"How many degrees does the minute hand of a clock turn in 25 minutes?", 150, L"The minute hand turns 360 degrees in 60 minutes, 6 degrees per minute: 25 \u00D7 6 = 150." },
{ L"How many degrees does the hour hand of a clock turn in 20 minutes?", 10, L"The hour hand turns 30 degrees per hour, 0.5 degrees per minute: 20 \u00D7 0.5 = 10." },
{ L"If the day after tomorrow is a Sunday, what day was the day before yesterday? Type 1 for Monday, 2 for Tuesday, \u2026, 7 for Sunday.", 3, L"Day after tomorrow is Sunday, so today is Friday. Two days before Friday is Wednesday." },
{ L"A 31-day month begins on a Saturday. How many Saturdays and Sundays does it contain in total?", 10, L"31 days = 4 weeks + 3 days, so Saturday, Sunday and Monday each occur 5 times: 5 + 5 = 10." },
{ L"A 24-hour digital clock shows 00:00 to 23:59. How many times a day are all four of its digits the same?", 3, L"Hours only go up to 23, so the only options are 00:00, 11:11 and 22:22." },
{ L"What is the largest possible number of Sundays in one calendar year?", 53, L"A year is 52 weeks plus 1 or 2 days, so one or two weekdays occur 53 times. A year starting on Sunday has 53." },
{ L"What is the smaller angle, in degrees, between the hour and minute hands of a clock at 3:30?", 75, L"At 3:30 the minute hand is at 180 degrees and the hour hand halfway from 3 to 4: 90 + 15 = 105. 180 \u2212 105 = 75." },
{ L"What is the smaller angle, in degrees, between the hour and minute hands of a clock at 7:20?", 100, L"At 7:20 the minute hand is at 120 degrees and the hour hand at 210 + 20 \u00D7 0.5 = 220. 220 \u2212 120 = 100." },
{ L"From first stroke to last, a clock takes 5 seconds to strike 6 o'clock. How many seconds does it take to strike 12 o'clock?", 11, L"6 strikes have 5 gaps, so each gap is 1 second. 12 strikes have 11 gaps: 11 seconds." },
{ L"Today is Wednesday. What day of the week will it be 1000 days from today? Type 1 for Monday, 2 for Tuesday, \u2026, 7 for Sunday.", 2, L"1000 = 7 \u00D7 142 + 6, so move 6 days ahead from Wednesday, the same as 1 day back: Tuesday." },
{ L"January 1 of a non-leap year is a Monday. What day of the week is December 31? Type 1 for Monday, 2 for Tuesday, \u2026, 7 for Sunday.", 1, L"365 = 52 \u00D7 7 + 1, so December 31 is 364 days, exactly 52 weeks, after January 1: also a Monday." },
{ L"A clock gains 15 minutes every hour. It was set right at noon and now shows 5:00 pm. How many minutes have really passed since noon?", 240, L"The clock shows 75 minutes for every 60 real ones. It shows 300 minutes, so 300 \u00D7 60/75 = 240 real minutes." },
{ L"How many seconds are there in one week?", 604800, L"60 \u00D7 60 \u00D7 24 \u00D7 7 = 3600 \u00D7 168 = 604800." },
{ L"From noon up to (but not including) midnight, how many times do the hour and minute hands of a clock point exactly the same way?", 11, L"The minute hand laps the hour hand every 12/11 hours, so 11 meetings in 12 hours: noon plus 10 more before midnight." },
{ L"In one 24-hour day, how many times do the hour and minute hands of a clock point in exactly opposite directions?", 22, L"Opposite happens once per lap of the minute hand over the hour hand: 11 times every 12 hours, so 22 times a day." },
{ L"A clock strikes the hour (once at 1 o'clock, \u2026, 12 times at 12 o'clock) and once at every half hour. How many strikes from 00:00 to 23:59?", 180, L"Hour strikes: 1 + 2 + \u2026 + 12 = 78, twice a day makes 156. Half-hour strikes add 24. Total 156 + 24 = 180." },
{ L"A 12-hour digital clock shows times from 1:00 to 12:59. How many of these times read the same backwards, like 3:43 or 12:21?", 57, L"For h:mm the last digit must equal h and the middle can be 0 to 5: 9 \u00D7 6 = 54. Add 10:01, 11:11, 12:21 for 57." },
{ L"In a month, three Sundays fell on even dates. What day of the week was the 20th? Type 1 for Monday, 2 for Tuesday, \u2026, 7 for Sunday.", 4, L"Sundays alternate odd and even dates, so 3 even ones need Sundays on 2, 9, 16, 23, 30. The 20th is 4 days after the 16th: Thursday." },
